# 4.1 Parametric Functions (Notes & Packet)

## Master Theory & Definitions
A **parametric function** in two dimensions defines the coordinates \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) as independent scalar functions of a third variable called a **parameter**, denoted by \\(t\\) (often representing time) or an angle parameter \\(\theta\\):
\\[f(t) = (x(t), y(t)) \quad \text{or} \quad \vec{r}(t) = \langle x(t), y(t) \rangle\\]

* **Component Functions**:
  * \\(x(t)\\): Determines horizontal displacement as a function of parameter \\(t\\).
  * \\(y(t)\\): Determines vertical displacement as a function of parameter \\(t\\).
* **Coordinate Mapping**: Evaluating the component functions at a specific parameter value \\(t = t_0\\) produces an ordered pair \\((x(t_0), y(t_0))\\) in the \\(xy\\)-plane.
* **Orientation and Tracing**: As \\(t\\) increases continuously across its domain, the ordered pairs \\((x(t), y(t))\\) trace out a planar curve. Directional orientation arrows along the curve indicate the path taken as \\(t\\) increases.
* **Domain Restrictions**:
  * If no interval is specified for \\(t\\), the domain of \\(t\\) is the intersection of the domains of \\(x(t)\\) and \\(y(t)\\).
  * When \\(t\\) is restricted to a closed interval \\(a \le t \le b\\), the graph is a bounded segment with initial point \\((x(a), y(a))\\) and terminal point \\((x(b), y(b))\\). **Endpoints do NOT receive arrows** when a domain restriction is explicitly given.

